The most notable of the 20th century's three founding families was Shun-fu, who is now under the legal title of Shu Ji, and has been long considered one of the most important emperors of Korea. A grandson of Shun-fu is Parkong-geo, the son of Parkong-geo, who is the grandson of Yasun-geo. In 1950, the government abolished the title of "Shun-fu" and added the title of "Shun-fu" to the title. However, the Japanese government has used the title of "Fukun-geo" and "Moo-shun" to the exclusion of the term of "Shun-fu".
